6.text:
For the $d$--cross

$$C_{d}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{d}[-1,1]e_{k},\qquad 
X_{i}=R_{i}e_{K_{i}},\;\;K_{i}\stackrel{iid}{\sim}{\rm Unif}\{1\ldots d\},
\;R_{i}\stackrel{iid}{\sim}{\rm Unif}[-1,1] $$
and $S_{d}=\{0,X_{1},\dots ,X_{d}\}$ put
$V_{d}={\rm Vol}_{d}\big({\rm conv}S_{d}\big)$.
On the event $\{K_{1},\dots ,K_{d}{\rm \;distinct}\}$,
${\rm P}\{K_{1}\dots K_{d}{\rm \;distinct}\}=d!/d^{d}$ and
$|{\rm det}[X_{1}\dots X_{d}]|=\prod_{i}|R_{i}|$ so that with
$E|R|=1/2$

\begin{equation*}
E V_{d}= \frac{d!}{d^{d}}\frac{\big(E|R|\big)^{d}}{d!}
       =\Bigl(\frac1{2d}\Bigr)^{d},\qquad E V_{1}= \tfrac12 ,
\end{equation*}
the classical $(2d)^{-d}$ volume.  Put
$m_{0}=E\sqrt{R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}}$ with $R_{i}\sim U[-1,1]$.
With $U,V\sim U[0,1]$

\begin{equation*}
m_{0}=\iint_{[0,1]^{2}}\!\sqrt{u^{2}+v^{2}}\,du\,dv
     =\frac{\sqrt2+\operatorname{arsinh}1}{3}
     =\frac{\sqrt2+\log \!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)}{3}\approx0.765196 .
\end{equation*}
On the same arm $E|R_{i}-R_{j}|=2/3$ and on two different
orthogonal arms $E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|=m_{0}$, hence

\begin{equation*}
E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|
   =\frac1d\frac23+\Bigl(1-\frac1d\Bigr)m_{0}
   =\frac{(n-1)m_{0}+2/3}{n}.
\end{equation*}
With $E\|X_{i}\|=1/2$ the mean of the $\binom{d+1}{2}$ unordered
pairs of $S_{d}$ is

\begin{equation*}
\bar D_{d}
   =\frac{d\frac12+\binom d2E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|}
          {\binom{d+1}{2}}
   =\frac{\,d+2(d-1)/3+(d-1)^{2}m_{0}\,}{d(d+1)}\;,
\qquad\bar D_{1}= \tfrac12 .
\end{equation*}
Hence the $d$--wise volume to mean–pairwise–distance ratio on the
whole set $\{0,X_{1}\dots X_{d}\}$ is

\begin{equation*}
R_{d}^{i}
   =\frac{EV_{d}}{\bar D_{d}}
   =\frac{d(d+1)}{d+2(d-1)/3+(d-1)^{2}m_{0}}
     \Bigl(\frac1{2d}\Bigr)^{d},\qquad R_{1}^{i}=1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in closed form

\begin{equation*}
R_{d}^{i}
   =\frac{d(d+1)(2d)^{-d}}
          {\,d+\tfrac{2(d-1)}3+(d-1)^{2}
           \dfrac{\sqrt2+\log(1+\sqrt2)}3\,}\; .
\end{equation*}
Numerically

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
d&R_{d}^{i}\\\hline
1&1.000000\\
2&0.10928\\
3&0.00751\\
4&0.000379\\
5&1.51\times10^{-5}
\end{array}
\]

and $R_{d}^{i}=O\big((2d)^{-d}\big)$.  Hence

\[
S^{i}=\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}^{i}
   =1+0.10928+0.00751+0.00038+\cdots\approx1.1172,
\]

i.e. $S^{i}=1.117\!-\!1.118$ to the quoted three figure accuracy,
$R_{1}=1$ being the trivial one–dimensional term and the whole
non–trivial tail $d\ge2$ being $0.1172\ldots$.  With the usual
sample–sample normalisation $E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|$ this is
$\sum_{d\ge1}(2d)^{-d}/E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|\approx0.844$.

Remark.  With the conventional distinct–axis, one point per arm,
normalisation $X_{i}=U_{i}e_{i},\;U_{i}\sim U[-1,1]$,

\[
EV_{d}^{p}=2^{-d}/d!,\qquad 
\bar D_{d}^{p}=\frac{1+(d-1)E_{s}}{d+1},\;
E_{s}=m_{0},
\]

so that $R_{d}^{p}=(d+1)2^{-d}/\{d!\,[1+(d-1)E_{s}]\}$ and

\[
S^{p}=\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}^{p}
     =1+\frac{3\,2^{-2}}{2\big(1+E_{s}\big)}+\cdots\approx1.2497\approx1.250,
\]

the $1.250$ “one–point–per–axis’’ value, $R_{1}^{p}=1,\;
R_{2}^{p}\approx0.21244,\;R_{3}^{p}\approx0.03293$, etc., often quoted
in the literature.  The $1.117$ value above is the i.i.d. on the whole
cross, origin–included, mean–pairwise–distance normalisation.

